{"text":"HSTU already provides a natural temporal extension point: learned relative position/time biases inside its SiLU attention, followed by multiplicative gating of aggregated values. These mechanisms can learn temporal relevance without replacing the causal backbone. The original paper supports this architecture, but does not establish that recency alone distinguishes persistent preference from completed purchase intent. [HSTU, 2024](https://arxiv.org/html/2402.17152v3). A 2025 generative-ranking paper also retains temporal information while investigating cheaper alternatives to quadratic relative biases, highlighting their computational cost. [Towards Large-scale Generative Ranking](https://genai-personalization.github.io/assets/papers/GenAIRecP2025/9_Huang.pdf).

Purchase-cycle research suggests avoiding a single monotonic decay: MgRIA reports repeated-purchase intervals combining a power-law trend with weekly/monthly periodic spikes. These findings concern repurchase tasks; transfer to unseen-item recommendation remains uncertain. [MgRIA, 2026](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1567422326000037). More directly, SAM models post-purchase satiation through time-sensitive suppression/recovery gates and a time-to-next-purchase auxiliary objective. Its mechanism depends on click/purchase distinctions and repeat histories that this benchmark may not supply. [Learning to Forget, 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.12714).

Experiment hypothesis: retain one jointly trained HSTU per category; add bucketed elapsed-time attention biases and a small internal gate conditioned on observed inter-event gaps and causal hidden states. Let the gate modulate recent versus broader historical information, without imposing universal decay or claiming observed purchase completion. Preserve causal masking and final nonpadding readout for left-padded histories. Use only timestamps available at prediction; never supply the held-out target timestamp. Evaluate temporal bias and gating through separate ablations against the corrected baseline."}
